WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:03.000
[upbeat rock music]

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.833
woman: Cloud Speech API
allows application developers

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:09.333
to use Google's best-in-class
speech recognition technology

00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:11.233
for a simple API.

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:14.133
You can send audio data
directly to the cloud

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:17.133
from your application
and the API will respond

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:19.766
with a text transcription
of your audio.

00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:23.900
Cloud Speech API uses the same
machine learning technology

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.900
we use for the advanced
voice recognition features

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.033
on Android devices, such as
voice search in the Google app

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:33.766
and voice typing
in Google Keyboard.

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:34.933
[phone chimes]

00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:36.866
What's the weather in Tokyo?

00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:38.366
[phone chimes]

00:00:38.366 --> 00:00:40.700
digital female voice: It's
8 degrees and clear in Tokyo.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.033
woman: The API supports
over 80 languages and provides

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:47.766
high recognition accuracy,
even in noisy environments.

00:00:47.766 --> 00:00:51.000
We encapsulate sophisticated
speech recognition models

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.166
built on years
of Google research

00:00:53.166 --> 00:00:57.300
with an easy-to-use API
for any app developer.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.333
So any application
can now listen

00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:01.866
and implement voice interactions
with their users

00:01:01.866 --> 00:01:04.833
using the power
of cloud computing.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:07.366
Let's see the Speech API
in action by calling it

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:11.233
directly from a Raspberry Pi
GoPiGo robot.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.666
The bot can capture audio
through its microphone,

00:01:17.666 --> 00:01:19.533
send it to the cloud,
and receive

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:22.433
the recognized text in response
in real time

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:26.066
with partial results returned
while the user is speaking.

00:01:26.066 --> 00:01:28.466
Now let's see how it works.

00:01:28.466 --> 00:01:30.466
Can you dance?

00:01:35.766 --> 00:01:38.466
You can also specify
to the API what language

00:01:38.466 --> 00:01:40.333
you would like to recognize.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.333
[speaking Japanese]

00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:47.433
[speaking Portuguese]

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.666
You can combine Speech API

00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:54.866
with the other
Google Cloud APIs.

00:01:54.866 --> 00:01:57.833
In our bot, we have combined it
with Vision API

00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:00.800
to run OCR on an image of text.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.800
Can you read this?

00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:11.733
Speech API supports
both streaming recognition

00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:13.633
and non-streaming recognition.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.766
That means the API
is best suited not only

00:02:16.766 --> 00:02:19.666
for real-time recognition,
like I demonstrated,

00:02:19.666 --> 00:02:22.266
but also for batch recognition
use cases

00:02:22.266 --> 00:02:26.700
such as transcribing voicemails,
podcasts, call center calls,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.466
or processing
any stored voice data.

00:02:29.466 --> 00:02:33.200
As a result, the Speech API
lets you add an interactive

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.133
and voice-activated interface
to your application

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:38.833
with the power
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:41.666
Please go to
cloud.google.com/speech/

00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:43.433
for more details.

